now-it-all (crew cooperation, professionalism). No explicit stress resilience, reliability unknown.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. B has clear stress resilience and conscientiousness, but interpersonal issues. C has strong technical and leadership but lacks stress evidence and has interpersonal concerns. A has calm under crisis and technical, but communication issues and egocentric. D has responsibility but hot temper.\n\nWhich candidate best balances? B seems strong on core safety criteria (stress, reliability, technical). Interpersonal issues are concerning but could be mitigated? Show-off and not open to new ideas also concerning. Which is more severe? B's nasty remarks could directly harm crew cooperation. C's show-off and not open to new ideas also harmful. Both have interpersonal concerns.\n\nWe might consider that B's stress resilience is proven, while C's is not. Stress resilience is critical for long-haul. Also B's conscientiousness indicates reliability. Memory for numbers is a weakness but maybe less critical than stress. So B may be the best overall.\n\nThus we may align with Agent 3 and vote for B.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: agree with Agent 3's shift, note that B's strengths outweigh concerns, perhaps mention that A and D have insufficient evidence.\n\nProvide explicit facts: B handles stress well, very conscientious, good at weather assessment, computer skills; but also nasty remarks. Argue that while interpersonal issues exist, they are less detrimental than lack of stress resilience.\n\nThus vote B.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON."}
